+"# Executing canonical source boundaries\n\nThis subordinate execution guide does not create a second architecture authority. Resolve/hash-check spec/architecture/README.md -> baseline.md; business tasks read canonical \u00a73/\u00a710 SRC-01 through SRC-07/\u00a711. These sections decide responsibility, root assembly/build/test whitelist, minimal shared support, import direction and separate backend placeholder expiry.\n\nallowed_paths limits writes and cannot waive higher authority. Ordinary tasks name service responsibility areas and presently required specific tests/assembly files. Escalate conflicts before implementation. A temporary migration records actual source/destination file/responsibility map, authorization, scope and acceptance exit before edits. No post-implementation architecture legalization.\n\nAcceptance combines canonical behavior/security/transaction fixtures with applicable ownership/import checks. Inspect actual logic and runtime graph: directory names and passing behavior cannot prove responsibility. Shared support cannot hide complete Auth/session-write business. Backend equivalence never exempts these rules; the Minimality Contract justifies only presently necessary interfaces/dependencies.\n\n## Current transition and recovery\n\nStage001 is bounded done under v1.1. Stage002 propagates execution inputs. Stage003 must implement and independently verify effective ownership/dependency/task/trigger checks with negative controls and Java stage fixtures. These tools are bounded accepted at2afeac8/run36726926394: ci/check_architecture.py --scope all --json and tests/architecture, with evidence003/hosted-acceptance.md; Go remediation product candidate d0ae52f5615320790ae7039cb48831873de6f486 is independently reviewed and accepted by hosted run36744072690; evidence004/final-hosted-acceptance.md. Historical stage003 source_go/Gate FAIL was expected before migration and remains preserved. Applicable source/dependency checks remain mandatory; no gran